Abstract

The proliferation of autonomous AI agents across enterprise and cloud infrastructure transitions large language models (LLMs) from passive advisors to active, state-changing operators. This paradigm shift introduces severe system-level vulnerabilities, primarily Excessive Agency (OWASP LLM06:2025). Current defense mechanisms rely on application-level prompt guardrails and declarative policy abstractions (e.g., OPA/Rego, Kubernetes Network Policies). However, declarative policies suffer from a critical flaw: they declare intent at the control plane but do not guarantee execution at the data plane, often resulting in CNI (Container Network Interface) datapath dependencies and policy bypasses. This paper presents the architectural formalization of an Autonomous Authority Infrastructure that enforces strict separation between AI reasoning and execution authority. By utilizing deterministic datapath enforcement at the tool-call boundary, the system governs agent actions through cryptographic source identity and non-compensatory containment mechanics. We analyze the limits of declarative policy abstractions and demonstrate how kernel-level enforcement (eBPF, audit, and PCAP observation) is required to reconcile authorized intent with observed network reality.

