3. Cryptographic Source Identity and Attribution

To prevent impersonation and man-in-the-middle tampering between the agentic reasoning loop and the execution kernel, every action must carry verifiable provenance.

· Provenance Generation: When an agent formulation is finalized, the orchestrator generates a canonical JSON representation of the action payload, hashed via SHA-256: H = SHA-256(Payload)
· Asymmetric Signing: The origin node signs the hash using an isolated Ed25519 private key scoped exclusively to that agent instance session: S = Sign_Kpriv(H)
· Verification Gate: The datapath enforcement engine validates S against the pre-registered public key K_pub before inspecting policy rules. If a signature mismatch occurs, the request is dropped instantly without parser overhead.

3.1 Tamper-Evident Audit Artifacts

Every processed transaction outputs a cryptographically chained audit log entry. Each log block includes the hash of the preceding block, creating an immutable ledger of autonomous authority utilization. This satisfies compliance and audit mandates (such as SOC2, ISO 27001, and emerging AI safety regulations) without introducing performance bottlenecks.

