4. Non-Compensatory Containment Mechanics

A foundational vulnerability in modern security systems is compensatory control drift, where an administrator introduces secondary privileges to resolve a primary blockage (e.g., "If the token is expired, fall back to default role").

4.1 The Fail-Closed Invariant

Our architecture enforces Non-Compensatory Containment. Safety checks are orthogonal and non-negotiable. If any single validation gate fails:

· Semantic boundary check fails -> DENY
· Cryptographic signature invalid -> DENY
· Rate limit or scope quota exhausted -> DENY

No subsequent heuristic override or LLM-driven self-correction loop can compensate for a primary failure. The system does not attempt to "guess" user intent; it halts execution and emits an immediate kernel-level telemetry alert.

