5. Network Boundary Semantics and Kernel-Level Enforcement

Relying solely on user-space or container-native abstractions introduces critical failure modes when securing high-consequence autonomous workloads.

5.1 Limits of Declarative Policy Abstractions

Standard Kubernetes deployments utilize Container Network Interfaces (CNIs) to enforce policies via declarative YAML manifests. While powerful for static microservices, these abstractions exhibit critical failure modes for autonomous AI agents:

1. Control Plane Latency: CNI controllers compile rules and push them down asynchronously. During high-velocity agent loops, an agent can execute dozens of rapid API calls before the CNI control plane converges.
2. Granularity Gaps: Standard CNI policies operate primarily at Layer 3/4. They cannot inspect the semantic content of a decrypted payload to verify whether an LLM-generated SQL query contains unauthorized parameter tampering.

5.2 Kernel-Level Telemetry and eBPF Enforcement

To bypass the limitations of declarative CNI layers, our architecture implements kernel-level observation utilizing Extended Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF) and packet capture (PCAP) verification primitives.

· Socket-Layer Interception: By attaching eBPF programs to sock_ops and cgroup/connect hooks, the enforcement layer inspects TCP/UDP payloads at the kernel socket buffer before packets hit the network interface.
· Deterministic Syscall Auditing: File system and process execution syscalls (execve, openat) are monitored via kernel tracepoints. If an agent attempts to invoke unauthorized binaries, the kernel module terminates the thread group instantly (SIGKILL), bypassing user-space exception handling.

